  "narrative": "During the 2010s, Black and African women's hairstyles and hair culture flourished with a renewed emphasis on embracing natural textures. The natural hair movement gained momentum, encouraging individuals to ditch chemical relaxers and heat-straightening in favor of unmanipulated presentation of textured hair, including waves, curls, and kinks. Hairstyles such as afros, cornrows, dreadlocks, and Bantu knots became increasingly popular, with many women embracing their natural textures as a symbol of identity and self-love. Protective styles like box braids and locs were also favored for their ability to minimize manipulation and exposure to environmental elements, promoting hair health and well-being. These hairstyles not only celebrated African heritage but also served as a means of resistance against Eurocentric beauty standards that had long marginalized Black women's beauty.",
